#
# Port Qt 4.8.7's OpenSSL backend to the 1.1.0+ API.
#
# What it changes
#   src/network/ssl/ - qsslsocket_openssl_symbols{.cpp,_p.h}, qsslcertificate.cpp,
#   qsslkey.cpp, qsslsocket_openssl.cpp. Nineteen compile errors, of two kinds.
#
#   Signatures that moved:
#     BIO_s_mem()      now returns a const BIO_METHOD*, and BIO_new() takes one.
#                      Both have to move together, or the error simply relocates
#                      to every q_BIO_new(q_BIO_s_mem()) call site.
#     CRYPTO_free()    grew file and line arguments.
#     SSL_CTRL_OPTIONS gone; SSL_CTX_set_options() is a real function now.
#     SSLv2_*_method() removed outright, SSLv3_*_method() present only in a
#                      library built with enable-ssl3-method. Both return null
#                      here, so QSslSocket fails to build a context for those
#                      protocols - which is what "not available" has to look
#                      like. Neither should come back: SSLv2 and SSLv3 are
#                      broken, not merely old.
#
#   Structs 1.1.0 made opaque, where a field read becomes an accessor. These are
#   the ones to review, because an accessor is not always an exact synonym:
#     x509->cert_info->version       -> X509_get_version()
#     x509->cert_info->serialNumber  -> X509_get_serialNumber()
#     x509->cert_info->key           -> X509_get_X509_PUBKEY()
#     pkey->type                     -> EVP_PKEY_base_id()
#     rsa->n / dsa->p + BN_num_bits  -> RSA_bits() / DSA_bits(), which say what
#                                       the line was computing rather than how
#     cipher->strength_bits/alg_bits -> SSL_CIPHER_get_bits()
#     ctx->cert_store                -> SSL_CTX_get_cert_store()
#     cipher->valid                  -> dropped. There is no accessor and none is
#                                       needed: the list SSL_get_ciphers() returns
#                                       no longer contains entries flagged invalid.
#
# Why
#   Qt 4.8.7 was released in May 2015 against the OpenSSL 1.0 API. There is no
#   OpenSSL that is both still supported and buildable with it unpatched: 1.0.2
#   went end of life in December 2019 and 1.1.1 in September 2023, and the struct
#   opacity that blocks the build arrived in 1.1.0 - so "use an older OpenSSL"
#   only avoids the work by going all the way back to a library six years without
#   public fixes.
#
# Which versions this was checked against
#   Written against 1.1.1w and then compiled unchanged against **3.5.7**, the
#   version the Qt 5 and Qt 6 targets build. Zero errors, and the resulting
#   libQtNetwork.a carries the QSslSocket symbols. That is why the file is named
#   for the 1.1 API rather than for a version: 3.x keeps that API and only
#   deprecates it, so one patch covers both and the kit ships a single OpenSSL.
#
#   If a future 3.x removes what it currently deprecates, the failures will be in
#   the same two categories listed above, and the guard is already the right
#   shape to add a third branch to.
#
# Where it applies
#   Every Qt 4 target, and only where OpenSSL is linked rather than dlopened -
#   see the comment in the patch body. Every hunk is guarded on
#   OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER, so building against 1.0.x still compiles exactly what
#   upstream shipped.
#
# What this does NOT claim
#   That Qt 4's SSL is secure. This makes it build against a maintained crypto
#   library; it does not touch Qt 4's own protocol handling, certificate
#   validation or defaults, none of which have had a fix since 2015. The
#   improvement is real and partial, and should be described that way.
